James McElreath (Member, IEEE) received the B.S. degree in aerospace engineering and the Ph.D. degree in aerospace engineering from Texas A&M University, College Station, TX, USA, in 2019 and 2023, respectively. He is currently a Postdoctoral Researcher pursuing research in the field of astrodynamics. His research interests include the guidance of hypersonic vehicles, orbit determination, and orbit trajectory design and optimization.
David E. J. van Wijk (Member, IEEE) received the B.S. degree in mechanical engineering from Cornell University, Ithaca, NY, USA, in 2021. He is currently working toward the Ph.D. degree in aerospace engineering with the Land, Air and Space Robotics Laboratory, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX, USA. He was an Undergraduate Researcher with the Laboratory for Intelligent Systems and Controls, Cornell University. His research interests include robotics and safety-critical control for autonomous systems.
Manoranjan Majji (Senior Member, IEEE) received the B.E. (hons) in mechanical engineering from the Birla Institute of Technology and Science, Pilani, Rajasthan, India, in 2003, and the M.S. and Ph.D. degrees in aerospace engineering from Texas A&M University, College Station, TX, USA, in 2006 and 2009, respectively. He was a Research Associate working on computational vision with Texas A&M University, and an Assistant Professor of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering with the University at Buffalo, Getzville, NY, USA. He then joined the faculty of Texas A&M University, where is currently a Professor of Aerospace Engineering and also directs the Land, Air and Space Robotics (LASR) laboratory. He has a diverse background in several aspects of dynamics and control of aerospace vehicles with expertise spanning the whole spectrum of modeling, analysis, computations and experiments. His ten Ph.D. graduates are making valuable contributions in the academic, national laboratory, and industrial research establishments. In addition to being a scholar, he has a great deal of engineering experience developing software systems and embedded systems from OEM products. He holds a provisional patent on a simultaneous location and mapping software suite, and was awarded a patent for developing a novel omnidirectional robot. He has authored or coauthored over 150 publications in the areas of state estimation, astrodynamics, tensegrity systems, vision navigation, and system identification, where he has made fundamental contributions. Dr. Majji is an Associate Fellow of the American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics and a Fellow of the American Astronautical Society. He was the recipient of the Milton Plesur award for excellence in teaching from the University at Buffalo, SUNY, and the 2021 Dean of Engineering Excellence Award at Texas A&M, and a 2021 Career Initiation Fellowship of the Texas A&M Institute of Data Science. He is an Associate Editor for the Journal of Astronautical Sciences.
